PMM MULTIBOOT SELF-TEST UPDATE NOTE
===================================

Purpose of this update
----------------------
This update adds a bare-metal RISC-V RV32I integration test for the physical
memory manager (PMM).  The test constructs a Multiboot v1-compatible
information structure and memory-map entry in assembly, calls pmm_init with
that structure, and checks that the PMM correctly initializes and allocates
physical frames.

Important terminology
---------------------
The existing project implements a PMM, not a complete virtual memory manager
(VMM).  It manages physical 4 KiB frames using bitmaps.  A complete VMM would
also require page-table construction, writing satp, executing sfence.vma, and
testing virtual-address translation.  Those features are not added here.

Changed existing file
---------------------
PMM

One RV32I assembler error was fixed in pmm_free_block.

Previous instruction:
    andi t0, a0, 4095

Problem:
    andi accepts a signed 12-bit immediate only (-2048 to 2047), so 4095 is
    not valid for an RV32I assembler.

Replacement:
    li   t0, 4095
    and  t0, a0, t0

This retains the original purpose: test whether a physical address is aligned
to the 4 KiB page size.

Added files
-----------
1. pmm_selftest.S
   The assembly test program.

   It creates pmm_test_mboot_info, a Multiboot v1 information structure, and
   pmm_test_mmap, one Multiboot memory-map entry.  The entry describes this
   usable physical-RAM interval for QEMU virt:

       base = 0x80000000
       length = 0x00800000  (8 MiB)
       type = 1             (usable RAM)

   It calls:

       pmm_init(&pmm_test_mboot_info)

   It then checks all of the following:

   - pmm_init derives pmm_total_blocks as 0x80800.
   - pmm_bitmap is initialized to a nonzero address.
   - the first allocated frame is nonzero.
   - the second allocated frame is nonzero.
   - the two allocated frames are different.
   - pmm_allocated_bitmap marks an allocated frame as owned.
   - freeing a valid frame makes it available for first-fit reuse.
   - attempting to free the bitmap-reserved page does not release it.
   - an index equal to pmm_total_blocks is rejected as out of range.

   On success, it writes the result through QEMU virt's NS16550A UART at
   address 0x10000000.

2. pmm_test_layout.inc
   Defines assembly constants for:

   - struct pmm_test_state fields: expected, actual, checks_passed, and
     checks_failed.
   - relevant Multiboot v1 information-structure offsets.
   - Multiboot v1 memory-map entry offsets and size.

3. pmm_selftest.ld
   Linker script for QEMU virt.  It links the image at 0x80000000 and defines
   _kernel_end after the .bss section, aligned to a 4 KiB page.  This lets
   pmm_init safely place the PMM bitmaps after the test image.

4. Makefile
   Provides these commands:

       make selftest CROSS=riscv64-unknown-elf-
       make run

   The first builds pmm_selftest.elf with RV32I settings.  The second starts
   the image on qemu-system-riscv32 using the QEMU virt machine.

5. PMM_SELFTEST.md
   User-facing build instructions and a brief explanation of the test.

